--- body ---
## Context

`tickets/AGENTS.md` is a git-erg asset installed into every adopter repo and
wired into the agent's *resident* context — a bare `@tickets/AGENTS.md` line in
the project `CLAUDE.md`, or the pointer `erg install --inject-agents` writes.
Every byte is re-read at the start of every session, in every adopter repo.
Nothing budgets it: the asset machinery asks one question — pristine or locally
edited — and size is never consulted.

The 11-repo sweep tabulated in 0278 found exactly one repo that has ever edited
it: ImperialDragonHarness, at 8 005 bytes against a 2 141-byte shipped baseline,
roughly 2 000 tokens paid at every session start everywhere that content is
resident.

The interesting fact is *what* accreted, and it is the reason a plain ban would
be destructive: optimistic-ID collision recovery, the renumber-clear-of-the-
frontier rule and its counter-example, the per-PR `gh pr view` enumeration
(and why `gh pr list --json files` silently returns nothing), running `erg check`
against `origin/main` after a ticket PR merges, the cross-PR CI gate, artifacts
versus decision records, and the handoff-document section template. Almost none
of that is harness-specific. It is generic git-erg lore that had nowhere else to
live, so it went into the only file the agent was guaranteed to read.

The fix is to give it a home. `erg integration` already exists and already
prints long-form guidance on demand (7 816 bytes today), which is the same
pointer-and-payload split the harness uses for its own conditional rules.

## Relevant files

- `src/go/assets/AGENTS.md` — the shipped asset, 2 141 bytes; canonical source,
  never edit a deployed copy
- `src/go/bootstrap_assets.go` — embeds and maps the asset
- `src/go/integration.go` (or wherever `cmdIntegration` lives) — the on-demand
  channel that gains the migrated material
- `~/.claude/tickets/AGENTS.md` — the 8 005-byte edited copy the material is
  read *from*; READ ONLY from this ticket, it lives in another repo and is
  0906's to change

## Actions

1. Partition the harness copy into three buckets: generic git-erg lore, adopter-
   specific material (CI job names, local paths, harness-only conventions), and
   what is already in the shipped baseline.
2. Move the generic long-form material into the `erg integration` output.
3. Leave a one-line pointer in the shipped `AGENTS.md` naming the command that
   prints it. The shipped file must not grow by more than that pointer — a lean
   resident file is the point, not a side effect.
4. Give adopter-specific lore a declared home that the shipped asset points at,
   so the ban 0289 installs never forces a repo to discard local knowledge.

## Test

Assert on *content*, not exit code: after the move, `erg integration` output
contains the optimistic-ID collision recipe and the `gh pr view` enumeration
trap. Red step — write the assertion before the move and watch it fail.

A size assertion on `src/go/assets/AGENTS.md` guards Action 3: the shipped
asset stays within its current order of magnitude.

## Verification

- [ ] `erg integration` output contains the optimistic-ID collision recipe
- [ ] `erg integration` output contains the `gh pr list --json files` trap and
      the per-PR `gh pr view` enumeration that replaces it
- [ ] the shipped `assets/AGENTS.md` has not grown by more than a pointer
- [ ] the pointer names a command that actually exists and exits 0
- [ ] `make check` passes

## Invariants

- Core stays offline: nothing added here may make the forge layer load-bearing
  for `erg` core. The `gh pr view` material is documentation printed by a
  command, not a code path that shells out to a forge.
- ASCII-only under `src/go/`.
- The canonical asset is `src/go/assets/AGENTS.md`; never edit a deployed copy.

## Exit criteria

The generic lore is reachable from `erg integration` in a repo that has only
run `erg init`, the shipped asset is still lean, and no adopter-specific
material was discarded in the move — it has a declared home named by the
shipped file.

## Related

- 0277 — parent tracker; keeps Actions 1 and 6 and the harness-side verification
- 0289 — the hard error that makes this migration load-bearing; must land after
